# rule.require.watch-release-after-push

after every push (`git.commit.set --push` or `git.commit.push`), watch the release cycle at once and surface any ci failure; no fire-and-forget, and no wait for the human to ask:
```sh
rhx git.release --watch || rhx show.gh.test.errors
```
it watches ci until done; on a fail it shows the test errors. green, and the human said release → `rhx git.release --into prod --mode apply --watch`
`--watch` reads no meter and spends naught. `--mode apply` spends one use of the human's release stamp (`git.release.uses`); a push rides the commit grant, never the stamp
enforcement: push with no release watch after = blocker

